  <h2>Warm-up — Another Curious Table</h2>
  <p>Row and column headings are 98, 99, 100, 101 and 102. Think of something you notice or wonder, then share with a partner. Only the two question-mark cells are individual numeric anchors.</p>
  <table class="source-chart"><caption>First-party five-by-five addition table reconstruction</caption><thead><tr><th>+</th><th>98</th><th>99</th><th>100</th><th>101</th><th>102</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><th>98</th><td>—</td><td>197</td><td>—</td><td>199</td><td>—</td></tr>
  <tr><th>99</th><td>197</td><td>—</td><td>199</td><td>—</td><td>201</td></tr>
  <tr><th>100</th><td>—</td><td>?</td><td>—</td><td>?</td><td>—</td></tr>
  <tr><th>101</th><td>199</td><td>—</td><td>201</td><td>—</td><td>203</td></tr>
  <tr><th>102</th><td>—</td><td>201</td><td>—</td><td>203</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Explain symmetry, odd/even behavior and why one addend increasing while the other decreases preserves a diagonal sum. Explanations remain teacher reviewed.</p>
  <h2>Activity 1 — Compare Han and Elena</h2>
  <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(230px,1fr));gap:12px">
  <div style="border:1px solid #7d93a8;border-radius:8px;padding:12px"><h3>Han: partial sums</h3><p>367 + 231</p><p>8 ones + 90 tens-value + 500 hundreds-value = 598</p></div>
  <div style="border:1px solid #7d93a8;border-radius:8px;padding:12px"><h3>Elena: one digit per place</h3><p>367 + 231</p><p>8 ones, 9 tens, 5 hundreds = 598</p></div></div>
  <p>Explain why the two written formats show the same place-value computation; independent first-party text, not original publisher algorithm artwork.</p>
  <h2>Activity 2 — Compose New Units</h2>
  <p>For 365 + 182, Han records 7 + 140 + 400. Elena records 7 ones, 4 tens and a newly composed hundred, giving 547. With a partner, explain how 14 tens are recorded differently without changing value.</p>
  <ol><li>174 + 352</li><li>273 + 619</li><li>354 + 198</li><li>525 + 376</li></ol>
  <p>Show both the composed ten and the composed hundred where appropriate. Your teacher checks algorithm layout and reasoning, not only final totals.</p>
  <h2>Synthesis and sources</h2>
  <p>Discuss why each method works and how a new ten or hundred is recorded. The original 2021 Student Task Statements, original artwork, and publisher-only Cool-down are not asserted as reproduced.</p>
